    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • 2026 Paris Motor Show: Jeep Reveals Six-Model European Strategy for 2030, Renegade Is Set to Return\nEurope's Auto Industry Faces New Trade-Off Between Emissions Rules and Local Production\n18 photos\nPhoto: Ineos\nBritain could join the Made in Europe club for EU automotive support in the near future, and the kicker is that the United Kingdom could enjoy this membership without rejoining the European Union. There is, however, a catch. London may have to align with Brussels on trade measures targeting Chinese newcomers, and in the process, it may have to surrender certain freedoms to set its own import policy in exchange for access to Europe's industrial support schemes.\nReported by Reuters, the condition is part of a provisional bargain prompted by France and Germany. This deal could give automakers more flexibility over the incoming 2035 auto emissions regulations. Both Paris and Berlin have been trying to settle a dispute that pits Germany's preference for more room to sell internal combustion vehicles against France's push for European-centric manufacturing.\nThis emerging compromise links the goals of both parties instead of treating them as separate problems. Looking at the bigger picture, Made in Europe is less about European automotive pride than deciding where that extremely sweet public money goes. The proposed rules would affect products brought through subsidy schemes, public procurement, and public auctions.\nAs far as France is concerned, Paris has pushed for a narrow definition limited to the member states of the union. Germany has argued that countries closely integrated with European supply chains should qualify as well. Under the compromise in question, the proposed rules would cover the European Union's member states and permit other European partners that previously belonged to the single market to opt in, thus opening the door to the UK.\nFor British automakers like Bentley, MINI, Rolls-Royce, and Jaguar Land Rover, the opportunity is pretty obvious. So is the condition attached to it, though, for the draft calls for complete reciprocity and alignment with EU rules covering investments from third countries and trade defense. Put into practice, Britain would have to follow European restrictions on Chinese-built vehicles and all future measures affecting Chinese imports.\nPhoto: Renault Brexiters may find this awkward, especially when you remember that the UK's government has successfully resisted the pressure to copy EU tariffs on Chinese vehicles. France dropped its opposition to British inclusion earlier this week, but only with the condition that London aligns with the bloc's trade defense policy.\nAdvantages within the union are pretty clear, all the while Britain greatly nerfs its freedom to diverge on some trade measures. The emissions side of the deal is the most consequential for automakers, though its precise mechanisms have yet to be detailed. Under the current framework, the fleet-wide target from 2035 represents a 100 percent reduction in CO2 emissions against the EU's 2021 baseline.\nPer a December 2025 European Commission automotive package, the 2035 deadline may result in a 90 percent tailpipe emissions reduction. The remaining 10 percent would be compensated through low-carbon steel made in the union or alternative fuels. According to Reuters, the Franco-German plan would keep the 90 percent reduction target while removing the need for those additional compensating measures, making compliance less restrictive.\nFurthermore, this proposal would also leave more room for internal combustion and plug-in hybrid vehicles after 2035. While not a rollback of emissions rules or a straightforward protectionist pact, this proposal goes to show that both France and Germany care about their automotive industries a lot more nowadays than they did back when everyone thought that electric vehicles would reign supreme by 2030.\nVia // Reuters\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nEurope UK France Germany emissions\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Electric Chevrolet Corvette Coming, GM Doesn't Know When",
